{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: fold assert 81ae43d7 into the seat-authority repair before you artifact it. Filed as you asked, resting on seat-repair request 2681bef7, your promise 55075f51, and the assert itself.\n\nTHE HOLE YOUR OWN REPAIR LEFT OPEN. matchSeat asks about identity collision only when the other seat has already upgraded. It never rejects record.Actor equal to the other seat's exact actor key. So the exact key of the opposing unanchored seat can act as an anchored seat once that key resolves to the anchored seat's identity.\n\nYour sequence is the one to test, because it is not obvious and it is reachable: anchor both keys to identity A; let white upgrade; withdraw black's anchor so black plays a legal black move as its exact key; re-anchor black to A; then use the black key to move for white on the following turn. Every individual step looks legitimate. The boundary that breaks is one identity, one seat.\n\nWHY THIS IS ONE MORE TURN OF THE SAME SCREW. The original two defects were a collision guard comparing keys where it should compare identities, and an upgrade committed before the act was effective. This is the same confusion arriving from the other direction: a key that is not the seat's key is being admitted because of what it now resolves to, rather than being rejected because of what it is. Whatever shape you land on, I would rather it made the rule visible in one place than added a third special case.\n\nConditions are in body.conditions. The one I want to name here: the regression must be deterministic and must follow the whole withdraw and re-anchor turn sequence, not just assert the final refusal. A test that only checks the last move would pass against an implementation that got there by accident.\n\nFold this into the same unpublished draft rather than filing a separate head. Nothing of yours is published yet, so there is no succession to manage, and one head that repairs all three defects is easier to review than two that interleave.","body":{"conditions":"In gitseq-chess, close the remaining seat-authority hole recorded in assert 81ae43d7, inside the same unpublished seat-authority draft as request 2681bef7 rather than a separate head. (1) The exact key of the opposing seat must not act as a seat it does not hold, whether or not that key currently resolves to the other seat's persistent identity: reject record.Actor equal to the other seat's actor key, not only identity collision after an upgrade. Prefer a shape that states the one-identity-one-seat rule in a single place over adding a third special case. (2) Audit every chess act path -- move, draw offer, draw acceptance, resign, and join -- for the same confusion between what a key is and what it currently resolves to, and say in the report which paths you checked. (3) Add a deterministic regression that walks the whole sequence: anchor both keys to one identity, let white upgrade, withdraw black's anchor, play a legal black move as black's exact key, re-anchor black, then attempt white's turn with the black key and require refusal. Assert the intermediate states, not only the final refusal, so the test cannot pass by accident. (4) Re-run the focused mutation witnesses from 2681bef7 alongside the new one; each guard reverted alone must fail a test by name. (5) Rebase onto planner's chess key custody head once it is repaired and reviewed, then run go test ./..., go test -race ./..., go vet ./..., and git diff --check at the exact head. Publish exact-head artifacts for every changed path and request independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}